<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	The Watch target used to generate this file from INFOPLIST_KEY_* build
	settings. It is authored now because the runtime claim a Watch recording
	needs — WKBackgroundModes — is an array, and an array is awkward to express
	as a build setting. See docs/milestones/milestone-1.6.md, spike A.

	CFBundleIcons and NSAccentColorName are deliberately absent: the asset
	catalog compiler injects both from ASSETCATALOG_COMPILER_APPICON_NAME and
	ASSETCATALOG_COMPILER_GLOBAL_ACCENT_COLOR_NAME. That injection is what lets
	the Champagne accent reach the Watch with no Swift change at all (see
	docs/architecture/visual-identity.md), so if the accent ever disappears from
	the Watch, check the built Info.plist for NSAccentColorName before touching
	any code.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>$(DEVELOPMENT_LANGUAGE)</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Onbii</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Onbii records where a recording was captured, so you can remember where a conversation happened. Location is optional and travels with the recording to your iPhone.</string>
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Onbii records audio only after you tap Record, then transfers the original recording to your iPhone.</string>
	<!--
		WKBackgroundModes is deliberately not declared yet.

		The first field test lost twenty-five minutes of a walk because this app
		has no sanctioned claim on runtime once it stops being frontmost, so
		watchOS was entitled to suspend it and the recorder stopped. Declaring a
		mode is not a free fix: each value is a statement about what this app
		is, and the honest one is not yet established. Spike A measures what
		watchOS 27 actually grants, one configuration per run:

			<key>WKBackgroundModes</key>
			<array>
				<string>audio</string>          <!- - does watchOS honour this
				                                     for recording, or only
				                                     playback? - ->
				<string>mindfulness</string>    <!- - WKExtendedRuntimeSession - ->
				<string>self-care</string>      <!- - WKExtendedRuntimeSession - ->
			</array>

		Add exactly one at a time and compare the preserved file's measured
		duration against wall-clock elapsed. Do not ship a mode that the app
		cannot honestly claim; if none fits, the app should say plainly that it
		cannot hold a recording in the background rather than imply it can.
	-->
	<key>WKApplication</key>
	<true/>
	<key>WKCompanionAppBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>com.yepyr.onbii</string>
	<key>WKRunsIndependentlyOfCompanionApp</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
